  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 208 AM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThe long term beings with a mid-level ridge centered over the \nSouthern Plains building eastward toward our area through the \nfirst part of next week. A backdoor 'cold' front will push through\nthe CWA on Monday, setting up a modified wedge pattern over the \narea with breezy east to northeast winds through mid-week. We can\nexpect a decent reduction in our daytime high temperatures, \nranging from the mid 70s in our mountain counties to the low-to-\nmid 80s elsewhere, followed by a warming trend back into the upper\n80s to lower 90s through the remainder of the week.\n\nAs the ridge continues to migrate eastward through mid-week, we \ncan expect a general reduction in shower and thunderstorm chances \nacross the northern and central areas, with isolated to scattered \nafternoon thunderstorms confined mostly south of a line from \nColumbus to Macon, where greater instability remains. For the \nsecond half of the week, the mid-level ridge will retrograde back \nto the west, allowing an increase in gulf moisture and instability\nas surface winds shift back toward the southwest. This will also \nallow for an increase in diurnal thunderstorm chances area-wide as\nwe move into next weekend.",
